            %p
              Lifting this diagram, we see that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(\alpha)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(\alpha')
              must lift to morphisms with identical domain, so
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
              is well-defined on objects.
